{"conversation_evidence":{"admissible_pre_tfg_turns":[{"create_time":1686853498.254301,"event_index":2,"role":"user","source_node_id":"aaa2864a-6cd8-4024-8b96-efa1a39bfda5","source_parent_id":"82d7ce97-1875-4298-b12a-030a8a973218","source_record_index":2,"text":"in python, how get the result of exec()","turn_id":"turn_000002"}],"first_generation":{"artifacts":[{"candidate_id":"GTC_000003_000792_000910_3aaea4985dea0688","completeness":"complete","container":"markdown_fence","continuation_status":"none","detector":{"name":"generated-technical-content-v2","version":"2"},"detector_evidence":["complete_standalone_markdown_fence","braces","call_expression"],"end_offset":910,"fence_language":"python","kind":"source_code","legacy_artifact_id":"ARTIFACT_000003_001","order_within_response":1,"raw_text":"code = \"\"\"\na = 1\nb = 2\nresult = a + b\n\"\"\"\n\nlocals_ = {}\nexec(code, {}, locals_)\nprint(locals_['result'])  # Output: 3\n","raw_text_sha256":"3aaea4985dea068802e658cd42aeb830fabfd545fa347f685712a6e756a61494","source_event_index":3,"source_record_index":3,"source_response_id":"turn_000003","source_turn_text_sha256":"3453c461b90480bcf567132e47122c258bb3188fd3e7e537efab09010d59e20f","start_offset":792,"status":"candidate_requires_semantic_review"}],"identification":{"ambiguity_reason":"","artifact_refs":["GTC_000003_000792_000910_3aaea4985dea0688"],"boundary":"exclusive_before:turn_000003","candidate_artifact_ids":["GTC_000003_000792_000910_3aaea4985dea0688"],"family_id":"FGF_4e7703119bc974873cf4b1c2","family_id_provenance":{"algorithm":"sha256","canonical_input":{"artifact_ids":["GTC_000003_000792_000910_3aaea4985dea0688"],"case_id":"CASE_91E13A588000"},"canonicalization":"json-sort-keys-compact-utf8","case_id_included":true,"digest_hex":"4e7703119bc974873cf4b1c2fe1496ad3ca1948343b501c4c4ea5236f2887b2c","digest_prefix_length":24},"family_label":"Python exec result retrieval via a locals dictionary","rationale":"The sole candidate is a coherent Python example generated in the first assistant response, demonstrating assignment during exec() and retrieval of the result from the supplied locals dictionary.","response_event_index":3,"response_turn_id":"turn_000003","status":"complete","target_prompt_event_index":2,"target_prompt_id":"turn_000002"}},"first_generation_response":{"create_time":1686853525.61934,"event_index":3,"role":"assistant","source_node_id":"ce63e210-343f-4ac7-9009-b211986370d7","source_parent_id":"aaa2864a-6cd8-4024-8b96-efa1a39bfda5","source_record_index":3,"text":"In Python, the `exec()` function is used to execute dynamically created program, which is either a string or object code. If it is a string, the string is parsed as a suite of Python statements which is then executed. If it is an object code, it is simply executed.\n\nThe `exec()` function does not return any value, hence you can't directly get a result from it like you would from a function that returns a value. \n\nHowever, the `exec()` function optionally takes a globals and locals parameter (both must be dictionaries), which it uses for global and local variables during execution. If you want to get a result from exec, you can pass a dictionary for locals, execute an assignment statement, and then retrieve the result from the locals dictionary.\n\nHere is a small example:\n\n```python\ncode = \"\"\"\na = 1\nb = 2\nresult = a + b\n\"\"\"\n\nlocals_ = {}\nexec(code, {}, locals_)\nprint(locals_['result'])  # Output: 3\n```\n\nIn this example, the code to be executed is a string that declares two variables `a` and `b` and a third variable `result` which is the sum of `a` and `b`. After executing this code with `exec`, the `locals_` dictionary is updated with the values of `a`, `b`, and `result`, and you can get the value of `result` by looking it up in the dictionary.\n\nPlease note, using `exec()` can potentially make your code vulnerable to code injection if you are executing code that comes from an untrusted source or includes user inputs. It is generally best to avoid using `exec()` if possible.","turn_id":"turn_000003"},"target_developer_prompt":{"create_time":1686853498.254301,"event_index":2,"role":"user","source_node_id":"aaa2864a-6cd8-4024-8b96-efa1a39bfda5","source_parent_id":"82d7ce97-1875-4298-b12a-030a8a973218","source_record_index":2,"text":"in python, how get the result of exec()","turn_id":"turn_000002"}},"csv_extraction":{"context":[{"category":"context","evidence_spans":["in python, how get the result of exec()"],"item_id":"context_1","normalized_text":"The task concerns Python's `exec()` function.","source_roles":["user"],"source_turn_ids":["turn_000002"]}],"specificity":[{"category":"specificity","evidence_spans":["how get the result of exec()"],"item_id":"specificity_1","normalized_text":"The user wants to obtain the result of calling `exec()`.","source_roles":["user"],"source_turn_ids":["turn_000002"]}],"verification":[]},"identity":{"case_id":"CASE_91E13A588000","conversation_id":"0b7b9fec-30b4-40dd-b887-fb7328f303f2","conversation_url":"https://chat.openai.com/share/0b7b9fec-30b4-40dd-b887-fb7328f303f2","outcome_class":"PA","pr_conversation_correspondence":"yes","pr_number":1,"pr_url":"https://github.com/pyspark-ai/pyspark-ai/pull/1","repository":"pyspark-ai/pyspark-ai"},"methodology_version":"dir-tfg-v2","primary_repository_cutoff":"tFG","record_status":"frozen","schema_version":"stage-e-frozen-conversation-v1","scientific_provenance":{"eligibility":{"decision":"eligible","manifest_row":{"adjudication_required":"false","authoritative_target_identifier":"0d6560a526765ad7e37fe6ed6ce825c31d41c76f","candidate_target_identifier":"","canonical_repository_url":"https://github.com/pyspark-ai/pyspark-ai","case_id":"CASE_91E13A588000","ci_component_status":"not_assessed","discussion_component_status":"not_assessed","eligibility_contract_version":"post-stage-c-eligibility-v2","exclusion_reason":"","failure_category":"","failure_reason":"","final_scientific_eligibility":"eligible","first_generation_b